WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.266 --> 00:00:01.067 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Welcome.
-Thank you.

00:00:01.067 --> 00:00:02.369
It's been a while.
It's great to be back with you.

00:00:02.369 --> 00:00:04.604
-And I'm so always flattered
and honored that you come up

00:00:04.604 --> 00:00:05.538 line:92%
right after you finish your job,

00:00:05.538 --> 00:00:06.639 align:start size:94% position:6%
because you just
finished taping "Nightly."

00:00:06.639 --> 00:00:09.075 align:start size:94% position:6%
-I stay after school for you.
-Thank you so much.

00:00:09.075 --> 00:00:10.744 align:start size:94% position:6%
You know, we always
have this problem

00:00:10.744 --> 00:00:11.878 align:start size:94% position:6%
where stuff happens
late in the day.

00:00:11.878 --> 00:00:12.946 align:start size:94% position:6%
This seemed to be one
of those days

00:00:12.946 --> 00:00:14.614
where you probably had to cover
some of the Manafort trial

00:00:14.614 --> 00:00:16.950
and some of stuff that happened
in the later part of the day.

00:00:16.950 --> 00:00:18.084 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yeah, it feels like --
you know,

00:00:18.084 --> 00:00:20.320 size:78% position:39%
we used to talk about
a 24-hour news cycle.

00:00:20.320 --> 00:00:21.755 align:start size:94% position:6%
Now it's down to
about two hours.

00:00:21.755 --> 00:00:22.689 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yeah.
-And it's, you know,

00:00:22.689 --> 00:00:24.524
we always get the broadcast set
and we're feeling good about

00:00:24.524 --> 00:00:25.959 size:78% position:39%
where it is, and then
usually about 5:00,

00:00:25.959 --> 00:00:28.728 align:start size:94% position:6%
something falls out of the sky
and then we're, you know,

00:00:28.728 --> 00:00:29.896 line:92%
we're on to the next thing.

00:00:29.896 --> 00:00:31.231 align:start size:94% position:6%
-I assume you
and your team have gotten

00:00:31.231 --> 00:00:32.332 align:start size:94% position:6%
a little bit better
at handling it

00:00:32.332 --> 00:00:34.534
as this two-hour cycle continues
to get shorter and shorter?

00:00:34.534 --> 00:00:35.835 size:75% position:38%
-You know, as I say,
that's how we roll.

00:00:35.835 --> 00:00:38.171 align:start size:94% position:6%
That's what we do.
-That's wonderful to hear.

00:00:38.171 --> 00:00:40.340 align:start size:94% position:6%
This is the thing
that keeps coming up

00:00:40.340 --> 00:00:41.941 align:start size:94% position:6%
that I always want to
ask you about.

00:00:41.941 --> 00:00:43.777 align:start size:94% position:6%
You asked Donald Trump
a question,

00:00:43.777 --> 00:00:46.179 align:start size:94% position:6%
a pretty normal question
in an interview

00:00:46.179 --> 00:00:48.114 line:92%
about the firing of James Comey.

00:00:48.114 --> 00:00:49.916 line:92%
It is now the most replayed clip

00:00:49.916 --> 00:00:52.452 size:78% position:39%
because this is where
he basically admitted

00:00:52.452 --> 00:00:56.156
that he did fire Comey because
of the Russia investigation.

00:00:56.156 --> 00:00:58.258 align:start size:94% position:6%
Did you know
when that was happening,

00:00:58.258 --> 00:01:02.595
when he was giving that answer,
that it was newsworthy?

00:01:02.595 --> 00:01:04.164 align:start size:94% position:6%
-I knew the interview
was newsworthy.

00:01:04.164 --> 00:01:07.267 align:start size:94% position:6%
I know he was opening up about
something that, you know --

00:01:07.267 --> 00:01:08.835 size:75% position:38%
and it just happened
a few days earlier.

00:01:08.835 --> 00:01:11.137 align:start size:94% position:6%
From the time we booked
the interview and arranged it

00:01:11.137 --> 00:01:12.172 align:start size:94% position:6%
to the time we actually
sat down,

00:01:12.172 --> 00:01:14.908
the middle of that is when
the Comey firing occurred.

00:01:14.908 --> 00:01:17.477 size:78% position:39%
So we knew the thrust
of the conversation

00:01:17.477 --> 00:01:18.711 size:75% position:38% line:92%
would be about that.

00:01:18.711 --> 00:01:21.081 align:start size:94% position:6%
I wasn't surprised
by his forthrightness.

00:01:21.081 --> 00:01:22.816 align:start size:94% position:6%
That's who he is.
He tends to own things.

00:01:22.816 --> 00:01:25.151 size:75% position:38%
I'd interviewed him,
you know, going back

00:01:25.151 --> 00:01:29.289 align:start size:94% position:6%
to the "Apprentice" days,
so I wasn't shocked.

00:01:29.289 --> 00:01:30.423 line:92%
You know, he continued to talk.

00:01:30.423 --> 00:01:33.827 align:start size:94% position:6%
And I continued to try
and make sure I was clarifying

00:01:33.827 --> 00:01:35.995
because sometimes in interviews,
you know,

00:01:35.995 --> 00:01:36.830 line:92%
things happen very quickly

00:01:36.830 --> 00:01:38.364 size:84% position:42%
and someone will listen
to one little thing.

00:01:38.364 --> 00:01:40.100 align:start size:94% position:6%
"What did he mean?"
I wanted to make sure

00:01:40.100 --> 00:01:41.901 align:start size:94% position:6%
that I understood exactly,
you know,

00:01:41.901 --> 00:01:42.769 size:81% position:41% line:92%
what his position was.

00:01:42.769 --> 00:01:45.605
But I will say every journalist
wants their work

00:01:45.605 --> 00:01:46.906 size:75% position:38% line:92%
to have some impact.

00:01:46.906 --> 00:01:47.874 size:84% position:42% line:92%
It is a little surreal.

00:01:47.874 --> 00:01:49.709 size:78% position:39%
I'll sit in my office
sometimes and look up

00:01:49.709 --> 00:01:51.377 line:92%
and see clips of that again.

00:01:51.377 --> 00:01:53.046 align:start size:94% position:6%
-It's also interesting,
one of the reasons

00:01:53.046 --> 00:01:56.583 align:start size:94% position:6%
I think it endures as well,
not just from the information,

00:01:56.583 --> 00:01:58.151 line:92%
it was not a "gotcha" question.

00:01:58.151 --> 00:02:00.920 align:start size:94% position:6%
And it was a very politely
conducted interview,

00:02:00.920 --> 00:02:03.923
even when you interrupt
to ask for clarification.

00:02:03.923 --> 00:02:05.792 size:75% position:38%
It seems like you're
trying to help him

00:02:05.792 --> 00:02:08.027 size:88% position:44%
make the point he makes,
which is then the point

00:02:08.027 --> 00:02:09.696 align:start size:94% position:6%
that is being used against him
so often.

00:02:09.696 --> 00:02:12.198 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yeah, and I appreciate that.
[ Laughter ]

00:02:12.198 --> 00:02:13.066 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
I appreciate that.

00:02:13.066 --> 00:02:15.401
I mean, I tell people,
any journalist would have

00:02:15.401 --> 00:02:18.004
asked virtually the same
questions given the timing

00:02:18.004 --> 00:02:19.272 line:92%
of when that interview happened.

00:02:19.272 --> 00:02:20.473 size:81% position:41%
They were -- you know,
it was pretty obvious

00:02:20.473 --> 00:02:23.977
that we needed to understand
why this firing had occurred.

00:02:23.977 --> 00:02:27.647
And he was, you know, as I
suspected he would, he owned,

00:02:27.647 --> 00:02:29.616 align:start size:94% position:6%
you know,
his position on that.

00:02:29.616 --> 00:02:31.217 line:92%
And, you know, they were --

00:02:31.217 --> 00:02:32.919
ended up getting a little
more time than I thought.

00:02:32.919 --> 00:02:34.420 align:start size:94% position:6%
You know, sometimes in these
kind of interviews

00:02:34.420 --> 00:02:36.222 align:start size:94% position:6%
when you've got a big,
important person,

00:02:36.222 --> 00:02:36.990 line:92%
you're playing the clock.

00:02:36.990 --> 00:02:38.525
"Okay, I want to get this
question, but I better jump

00:02:38.525 --> 00:02:40.260 size:84% position:42%
to the next one because
I may run out of time."

00:02:40.260 --> 00:02:41.895 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Sure.
-This mental gymnastics

00:02:41.895 --> 00:02:42.862 line:92%
is going on the whole time.

00:02:42.862 --> 00:02:45.565 align:start size:94% position:6%
But in the end, you know,
we got a good amount of time

00:02:45.565 --> 00:02:46.966 line:92%
and got to explore a lot.

00:02:46.966 --> 00:02:49.169 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Do you think based on that,
he has hesitation

00:02:49.169 --> 00:02:51.738
of sitting down with you again,
because it went so badly

00:02:51.738 --> 00:02:53.873 align:start size:94% position:6%
for him in retrospect?
[ Laughter ]

00:02:53.873 --> 00:02:54.674 line:92%
-Oh, I mean, I don't know.

00:02:54.674 --> 00:02:57.310
I mean, you know, I don't know
whether he thinks it went badly

00:02:57.310 --> 00:03:00.513 align:start size:94% position:6%
or, you know --
it's not for me to judge.

00:03:00.513 --> 00:03:01.748 align:start size:94% position:6%
-He thinks it went badly
for you.

00:03:01.748 --> 00:03:03.816 align:start size:94% position:6%
He did tell me that.
[ Laughter ]

00:03:03.816 --> 00:03:04.717 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
-Well...

00:03:04.717 --> 00:03:06.819 align:start size:94% position:6%
I would obviously welcome
another chance

00:03:06.819 --> 00:03:07.854 size:78% position:39% line:92%
to sit down with him.

00:03:07.854 --> 00:03:09.756 line:92%
That opportunity has not --

00:03:09.756 --> 00:03:11.191 align:start size:94% position:6%
you know, has not
knocked on my door.

00:03:11.191 --> 00:03:13.393 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Well, you are --
I hate to break this to you --

00:03:13.393 --> 00:03:14.994 line:92%
you are a member of the media,

00:03:14.994 --> 00:03:18.631
and you, as a collective,
as has been explained to me

00:03:18.631 --> 00:03:20.433 align:start size:94% position:6%
by our President,
are an enemy of the people.

00:03:20.433 --> 00:03:24.003 align:start size:94% position:6%
And he's recently tweeted that
you are dangerous and sick.

00:03:24.003 --> 00:03:28.775
Is it strange to be representing
a profession right now

00:03:28.775 --> 00:03:30.276 align:start size:94% position:6%
that the President
of the United States

00:03:30.276 --> 00:03:34.280 align:start size:94% position:6%
is just full bore attacking
on a daily basis?

00:03:34.280 --> 00:03:35.348 size:84% position:42% line:92%
-You know, I let the --

00:03:35.348 --> 00:03:38.117 align:start size:94% position:6%
I let the "fake news" stuff
roll off my back.

00:03:38.117 --> 00:03:39.219 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
It's not fun.

00:03:39.219 --> 00:03:41.120 align:start size:94% position:6%
But I think most people
understand

00:03:41.120 --> 00:03:42.355 line:92%
an organization like ours,

00:03:42.355 --> 00:03:44.324 align:start size:94% position:6%
and a lot of the organizations
under fire,

00:03:44.324 --> 00:03:45.959 align:start size:94% position:6%
have been doing this
for decades and decades.

00:03:45.959 --> 00:03:48.761
And we've earned a lot of trust
with our audiences

00:03:48.761 --> 00:03:50.096 line:92%
and a tradition of excellence.

00:03:50.096 --> 00:03:51.898 line:92%
So I let -- I let that roll.

00:03:51.898 --> 00:03:54.901 align:start size:94% position:6%
I think where I draw the line,
though, is the safety

00:03:54.901 --> 00:03:56.402 line:92%
and well-being of my colleagues.

00:03:56.402 --> 00:04:00.306
And "enemy of the people"
I think crosses a line.

00:04:00.306 --> 00:04:04.777
I think it's reckless.
I respectfully ask that it end.

00:04:04.777 --> 00:04:07.113 align:start size:94% position:6%
And that's what I have to say
about it.

00:04:07.113 --> 00:04:09.015 size:84% position:42% line:92%
-I also respectfully...

00:04:09.015 --> 00:04:11.484 align:start size:94% position:6%
[ Cheers and applause ]
...ask that it end.

00:04:11.484 --> 00:04:13.553 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
And...

00:04:13.553 --> 00:04:16.456
I will say, I have some bad news
for both of us

00:04:16.456 --> 00:04:19.125 align:start size:94% position:6%
based on how respectfully
asking is working.

00:04:19.125 --> 00:04:21.160 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
[ Laughter ]

